When \(l\geqslant 2\) and \(\psi\in H^{l+3/2}(\Omega)\cap\,W^{l+1,\,\infty}(\Omega)\), \(p\in H^{l-1/2}(\Omega)\) the best estimate is:

\[\|u-u_{h}\|_{\bar{\boldsymbol{x}}} \leqslant Ch^{l-1/2}(\|\psi\|_{l+3/2,\,\Omega}+\,\|\psi\|_{l+1,\, \infty,\,\Omega}),\] \[\|p-p_{h}\|_{0,\,\Omega} \leqslant Ch^{l-1/2}(|p|_{l-1/2,\,\Omega}+\,\|\psi\|_{l+3/2,\, \Omega}+\,\|\psi\|_{l+1,\,\infty,\,\Omega}).\]

When \(l=1\), \(\psi\in W^{2,\,\infty}(\Omega)\), \(\varLambda\psi\) and \(p\in H^{1}(\Omega)\) the best estimate is:

\[\|u-u_{h}\|_{\bar{\boldsymbol{x}}} \leqslant Ch(|\varLambda\psi|_{1,\,\Omega}+h^{-1/2}\|\psi\|_{2,\, \infty,\,\Omega}),\] \[\|p-p_{h}\|_{0,\,\Omega} \leqslant Ch(|p|_{1,\,\Omega}+|\varLambda\psi|_{1,\,\Omega}+h^{-1/2}\| \psi\|_{2,\,\infty,\,\Omega}).\]

_Remark 3.2_.: When \(\psi\) belongs to \(H^{k+2}(\Omega)\) and not to \(W^{k+1,\,\infty}(\Omega)\), the estimates of Theorem 3.1 are nearly valid. More precisely, by applying Lemma 3.2 with this \(k\) and arbitrary \(p\geqslant 2\) and using Sobolev's Imbedding Theorem I.1.3 we can replace (3.6) by:

\[\inf_{w_{h}\in\,V_{h}}\|v-w_{h}\|_{\bar{\boldsymbol{x}}}\leqslant C_{p}h^{k-1/ 2-1/p}\|\phi\|_{k+2,\,\Omega}\ \ \ \ \forall p\geqslant 2.\]

Hence, by letting \(p\) tend to infinity, we see that for each \(\varepsilon>0\) there exists a constant \(C(\varepsilon)>0\) such that

\[\inf_{w_{h}\in\,V_{h}}\|v-w_{h}\|_{\bar{\boldsymbol{x}}}\leqslant C( \varepsilon)h^{k-1/2-\varepsilon}\|\phi\|_{k+2,\,\Omega}.\]

In turn, this implies the estimate

\[\|u-u_{h}\|_{\bar{\boldsymbol{x}}}\leqslant C(\varepsilon)h^{k-1/2- \varepsilon}\|\psi\|_{k+2,\,\Omega},\]

with no restriction upon \(k\) or \(\varDelta\psi\) as in this case \(\varDelta\psi\) belongs to \(H^{k}(\Omega)\) with \(k\geqslant 1\).

Here again, a standard density argument establishes that the "stream function-vorticity" method is convergent.

**Corollary 3.2**.: _Let \(\Omega\) and \(\mathcal{T}_{h}\) be like in Theorem 3.1 and let the solution \((\mathbf{u}=\mathbf{curl}\,\psi,\)\(p)\) of the Stokes problem (2.1) belong to \(H^{1}_{0}(\Omega)^{2}\times L^{2}_{0}(\Omega)\). Then the solution \((u_{h},p_{h})\) of Problem (2.29) (2.36) satisfies:_

\[\lim_{h\to 0}\left(\|u-u_{h}\|_{\bar{\boldsymbol{x}}}+\,\|p-p_{h}\|_{0,\, \Omega}\right)=0.\]

When studying Navier-Stokes equations in Chapter IV, we shall encounter right-hand sides \(\mathbf{f}\) with no better than \(L^{r}\)-regularity, \(1<r\leqslant 2\). Now since \(\Omega\) is assumed to be convex, the solution \((\psi,\omega,p)\) of the Stokes problem belongs to \(W^{3,\,r}(\Omega)\times\,W^{1,\,r}(\Omega)\times\,W^{1,\,r}(\Omega)\) whenever its right-hand side belongs to \(L^{r}(\Omega)^{2}\). In this case, we cannot apply directly (A.26) to evalute \(\|\omega-P_{h}\omega\|_{0,\,\Omega}\). Instead, we prove the following approximation result.

